  {
    "id": "ART006",
    "slug": "balinese-temple-etiquette",
    "title": "Essential Temple Etiquette: Respecting Sacred Spaces in Bali",
    "excerpt": "Learn the proper way to dress, behave, and participate when visiting temples in Nyalian and throughout Bali.",
    "content": "# Respecting Sacred Spaces\n\nBali's temples are living spiritual centers, not museums. Understanding and following proper etiquette shows respect for local culture and ensures you're welcome to experience these sacred places.\n\n## Dress Code Requirements\n\n### For Everyone\n\n- **Sarong** - A traditional cloth wrapped around the waist, covering legs to ankles\n- **Sash (selendang)** - A sash tied around the waist over the sarong\n- **Modest top** - Shoulders and chest covered, no tank tops or bare shoulders\n\n### Additional for Women\n\n- **Kebaya or modest blouse** - Traditional Balinese top or any modest shirt\n- **No entry during menstruation** - This traditional rule applies at most temples\n\n### Where to Get Temple Attire\n\nMany temples provide rental sarongs and sashes at the entrance. It's also worth purchasing your own set to keep, available at local markets.\n\n## Behavioral Guidelines\n\n### Do:\n\n- Remain quiet and respectful\n- Follow the guide's or pemangku's instructions\n- Step over temple thresholds (don't step on them)\n- Walk clockwise around temple grounds\n- Ask permission before taking photos\n- Remove shoes before entering inner sanctums\n- Make a small donation or offering if invited\n\n### Don't:\n\n- Point your feet at shrines or sacred objects\n- Sit higher than priests or religious leaders\n- Touch sacred objects without permission\n- Enter unless properly dressed\n- Use flash photography during ceremonies\n- Interrupt prayers or ceremonies\n- Show affection or argue on temple grounds\n\n## Temple Structure\n\nUnderstanding basic temple layout helps navigate respectfully:\n\n### Tri Mandala Concept\n\n1. **Jaba (Outer Courtyard)** - Public area, least sacred\n2. **Jaba Tengah (Middle Courtyard)** - Semi-sacred transition space\n3. **Jeroan (Inner Courtyard)** - Most sacred area\n\n## Participating in Ceremonies\n\n### As a Visitor\n\nIf you're invited to observe a ceremony:\n\n- Arrive on time\n- Sit quietly in designated visitor areas\n- Accept holy water if offered (cup hands and sip, wipe on head)\n- Accept blessed flower offerings (place behind ear or in hair)\n- Stay until appropriate to leave (ask your guide)\n\n### Photography Rules\n\n- Always ask permission first\n- No flash during ceremonies\n- Be discreet and respectful\n- Never photograph devotees during prayer without consent\n\n## Offerings (Canang Sari)\n\nIf you wish to make an offering:\n\n- Purchase canang sari from local women near temple entrances\n- Place offerings at shrines before entering\n- Face offerings toward the shrine\n- Sprinkle holy water if available\n- Pray briefly if you wish\n\n## Special Considerations\n\n### Odalan (Temple Anniversary)\n\nThese major ceremonies occur according to the 210-day Balinese calendar:\n\n- Temples are very busy and crowded\n- Extra strict dress codes may apply\n- Best for observation, limited exploration\n- Incredible cultural experience if you're respectful\n\n### Family Temples (Pura Kawitan)\n\nSome temples are restricted to specific family groups. Always check if visitors are welcome.\n\n## Why These Rules Matter\n\nBali's temples aren't attractions—they're living spiritual centers where people worship daily. Following etiquette ensures:\n\n- Respect for local beliefs and practices\n- Preservation of sacred atmosphere\n- Your welcome as a visitor\n- Positive cultural exchange\n\n## Final Tips\n\nWhen in doubt, observe what locals do and follow their example. Most Balinese people are gracious and understanding with respectful visitors who make an effort to honor their traditions.",
    "coverImage": "/images/Pura Puncak Sari_1.webp",
    "category": "Travel Tips",
    "author": "Nyalian Cultural Team",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-10T08:00:00Z",
    "tags": ["Temple Etiquette", "Balinese Culture", "Travel Tips", "Respect"],
    "featured": false,
    "readTime": "9 min read"
  }
]